<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">San José Mogote</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San José Mogote is an archaeological site in the Etla arm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-oaxaca">Valley of Oaxaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, the largest and most important village of the valley between about 1500 and 500 BCE. It has some of the earliest public buildings and carved monuments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, including a stone showing a sacrificed captive with an early calendar glyph. Around 500 BCE much of its population probably moved to found Monte Albán.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-jose-mogote.jpg" alt="San José Mogote" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maunus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Jose_Mogote_Pyramid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The site lies in the fertile bottomlands of the Etla arm of the valley, about 12.1 kilometres north-west of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>. It is considered the oldest permanent farming village of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-oaxaca">Valley of Oaxaca</a> and probably the first there to use pottery, and it was supported by smaller settlements such as San Pablo Huitzo, Santa Marta Etla, Tomaltepec and Tierras Largas. Kent Flannery and Joyce Marcus investigated it over two decades of the late 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Between 1500 and 1150 BCE it grew from a few houses into the largest of some 25 villages in the valley and the only one with public buildings. Households stored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> in bell-shaped pits, placed figurines with burials and obtained obsidian from outside the valley, while irrigation ditches and pot irrigation from shallow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> may have raised harvests. The site has produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s oldest known defensive palisades and ceremonial buildings, of about 1300 BCE, and a men's house oriented eight degrees west of north, as at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a>.</p><p>Between 900 and 600 BCE about half the valley's people lived there. Only San José Mogote made mirrors of polished magnetite, traded to the Olmec Gulf Coast some 400 kilometres away, and it also obtained turtle-shell drums, conch-shell trumpets and stingray spines. John E. Clark judges that by the end of the San José phase it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a>. Monument 3, set as the doorstep of a ceremonial building, bears the earliest known Zapotec glyphs, and Mound 1 was raised on a hill augmented to 15 metres.</p><p>When Monte Albán rose as the main city of the valley, San José Mogote became a lesser community under its control, and the public buildings on Mound 1 were rebuilt as the residence of a high-ranking family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins are surrounded by the present-day village of San José Mogote in the Mexican state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, and the site is studied as an early centre of Zapotec culture and a forerunner of Monte Albán. Artefacts from the excavations are shown in the village's Community Museum, housed in the El Cacique Hacienda.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “San José Mogote” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of Mexico, on the reliefs of Chalcatzingo in Morelos, in the painted caves of Juxtlahuaca and Oxtotitlán and at Teopantecuanitlan in Guerrero, at <b>San José Mogote</b> in the Valley of Oaxaca, and on carved boulders at Pijijiapan in Chiapas, Takalik Abaj in Guatemala and Chalchuapa in El Salvador.</blockquote><a href="/culture/olmecs">Olmecs · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of the phase the largest settlement, <b>San José Mogote</b>, lost most of its population.</blockquote><a href="/culture/zapotec-civilisation">Zapotec civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The move, probably by an alliance led from <b>San José Mogote</b>, created a capital that soon dominated the valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/monte-alban">Monte Albán</a></li></ul></section>
